n:short","courseId":"pronouns","skillId":"reflexivePronoun","type":"short","prompt":"Your friend writes “Dad and myself went fishing.” Explain the mistake and fix the sentence.","rubric":["Explains that “myself” can’t be the subject; it only works when I do something to myself or for emphasis","Rewrites it as “Dad and I went fishing.”"],"model":"“Myself” can’t do the action in a sentence. It is only used when I do something to myself, like “I hurt myself.” The sentence should be “Dad and I went fishing.”","text":"It should be \"Dad and I went fishing.\" Myself is reflexive, so it only works when I already said I or me earlier in the sentence, like \"I made myself a sandwich.\"","at":"@T3@10:20:00","where":"practice"},{"id":"wdemo1","qid":"pronouns:pronounCase:short","courseId":"pronouns","skillId":"pronounCase","type":"short","prompt":"Explain why “Me and Theo built a fort” is wrong, and fix it.","rubric":["Says the pronoun is part of the subject, so it should be “I”","Rewrites it as “Theo and I built a fort.”"],"model":"“Me” is an object pronoun, but here the pronoun is doing the building, so it needs the subject pronoun “I.” Theo and I built a fort.","text":"Me is an object pronoun, but in this sentence it is part of the subject. So it should be \"Theo and I built a fort.\" A trick is to take out Theo: \"I built a fort\" sounds right.","at":"@T1@10:21:00","where":"practice"}],"readingBooks":[{"id":"book-0","title":"Hatchet","author":"Gary Paulsen","chapters":[{"id":"ch1","label":"Chapter 1","characters":"Brian, the pilot","notableAction":"The pilot has a heart attack while Brian is flying alone with him.","interaction":"","conflict":"Brian has to figure out how to land the plane by himself.","joy":"I liked how it started right away with something big happening.","setting":"","themes":"Being brave when you are scared.","detail":"","vocabulary":"","submittedAt":"@T20@14:00:00","localReview":"verified","updatedAt":"@T20@14:00:00"},{"id":"ch2","label":"Chapter 2","cha